%I A399741 #11 Sep 14 2026 22:43:24
%S A399741 2,4,8,12,17,51,71,213,287,335,407,455,473,527,575,603,673,2019,2695,
%T A399741 2711,8133,10847,32541,43391,130173,173567,178295,178511,179951,
%U A399741 539853,719807,785255,942311,2826933,3769247,11307741,15076991,15161399,15242487,15255967
%N A399741 Starting with 2, each term is the sum, over all preceding terms, of the least and greatest prime factors of each term.
%C A399741 Let lpf(m) and gpf(m) denote respectively the least and greatest prime factors of m. The sequence is obtained by summing lpf(m) + gpf(m) over all preceding terms m.
%C A399741 If m is a prime power, then lpf(m) = gpf(m), and this prime factor is counted twice. In particular, the first term 2 contributes 2 + 2 = 4, giving a(2) = 4.
%C A399741 Equivalently, after the initial terms a(1) = 2, a(2) = 4, each new term is obtained from the preceding term by adding its least and greatest prime factors.
%F A399741 a(1) = 2, a(2) = 4. For n >= 2, a(n+1) = a(n) + A074320(a(n)).
%F A399741 Equivalently, for n >= 2, a(n) = Sum_{k=1..n-1} A074320(a(k)).
%e A399741 a(2) = 2 + 2 = 4, since both the least and greatest prime factors of 2 are 2.
%e A399741 a(3) = 4 + 2 + 2 = 8.
%e A399741 a(4) = 8 + 2 + 2 = 12.
%e A399741 a(5) = 12 + 2 + 3 = 17.
%t A399741 f[n_] := With[{p = FactorInteger[n][[All, 1]]},First[p] + Last[p]];Join[{2}, NestList[# + f[#] &, 4, 48]]
%Y A399741 Cf. A020639, A006530, A074320.
%Y A399741 Cf. A076271, A116917, A000458, A000792, A074867.
%K A399741 nonn,easy,new
%O A399741 1,1
